panvk: every IOSurface the device imported is named in each job chain's buffer list, as one it may read (Darwin only)

Applied by tools/mk-panvk.sh only, after tools/mesa/*.patch and panvk-host-memory.patch, to Mesa 25.0.7 (Mesa's own
licence, MIT, applies to the lines it adds). Written for Scion.

Why. HakiosMali cleans the lines a processor wrote into an IOSurface before a job only for the buffers the submission
names (scion-kernel drivers/gpu/mali, phase 8 of docs/metal.md). PanVK's JM submission names its pools, the framebuffer,
a blit's two ends and the tiler heap, never a buffer or image a shader samples: a texture over a surface the CPU wrote,
which the GPU only reads, was read stale (zeros in 5 of 6 runs of an 8x8 surface; docs/metal.md, "Real apps").

What. The device keeps the IOSurfaces it imported as memory (panvk_AllocateMemory, panvk_FreeMemory). Each batch names
those its own list does not with HAKIOS_DRM_BO_SAMPLED (tools/mesa/hakios-drm): the kernel cleans a surface's lines
before the job where its seed says a processor may have written them since the last clean (a seed comparison for the
others), keeps the buffer until the job is done, and does not hold the display back for a job that only reads the
surface. hakios-drm takes the bit off for a kernel that does not take it, which then names them as any other buffer.

